    "notes": "G421. The honest counterpart to the on-payday scenario: the same salary account, but the three standing orders are dated Thu 26 Nov, one day BEFORE the Fri 27 Nov salary. An outflow that lands before the income that funds it is a real gap in this window and is never hidden. Today is Wed 25 Nov, payday in 2 days, window 25 and 26 Nov. Every account is read in isolation, money in against money out; an own-account standing order is money out on its source and money in on its destination on its date, whether or not the source can afford it. Barclays 600 - 150 (council tax) - 1,600 (standing orders) = -1,150 after payments and at the low point, short. HSBC 150 + 900 = 1,050; NatWest 120 + 500 = 620; Monzo 80 + 200 = 280. Left to spend is the sum, -1,150 + 1,050 + 620 + 280 = 800, which equals the four balances less the council tax (950 - 150) because the transfers net to zero. Every standing order is an ordinary predicted payment, so Barclays is an ordinary shortfall. Cover plan (at most two sources): the amount needed is ceil5(1,150) + 10 = 1,160. Headroom is balance minus the 10 buffer: HSBC 140, NatWest 110, Monzo 70. No single source covers it, so the two largest are used and never a third: HSBC 140 and NatWest 110, 250 in all, leaving 900 short after cover. Spend from after the moves: Barclays -1,150 + 250 = -900, HSBC 1,050 - 140 = 910, NatWest 620 - 110 = 510, Monzo 280; the sum is 800 again. At risk count 4: every bounced item counts, own-account standing orders like any payment. Same-day items walk largest first (900, 500, 200, then the 150 council tax): 600 - 900 = -300 (the HSBC order bounces first), -800 (NatWest order), -1,000 (Monzo order), -1,150 (council tax). The card names the 900 HSBC order.",
